course: dictionary
lesson: 10929
title: Who is a voter?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A voter is a person who votes in an election, or is allowed to vote. Voting means choosing someone by marking a paper or putting up a hand, so a voter is someone who makes that choice. Look at the word: vote, plus -er, which often means a person who does something, like a runner or a writer.
explain: Word clues help. «In a democracy the voters choose the people who make the rules.» The sentence tells you that voters choose leaders, so a voter has a say in how a country is run. Ask yourself: who is doing the choosing here? That person is the voter.
explain: Children learn that adults over a certain age can vote, so you may not be a voter yet. You can still learn how voting works. «Voter turnout» means how many people actually vote in an election, and «first-time voters» are people voting for the very first time.
fact: A voter is a person who votes in an election or is allowed to vote.
fact: The ending -er often means a person who does something, so a voter is someone who votes.
fact: A floating voter changes which party they vote for; a loyal voter always votes for the same one.
fact: «Voter turnout» means how many people actually vote in an election.
fact: In Australia, «voters number the candidates in order of preference», which means they rank them 1, 2, 3 and so on.
exercise: «In a democracy the voters choose the people who make the rules.» Who are the voters? | *People who are allowed to vote in an election | The leaders who already make the rules | The people who count the votes | The people who stand as candidates
exercise: What is a loyal voter? | *A voter who always votes for the same party | A voter who never votes | A voter who changes party every time | A voter who counts the votes
exercise: «An exit poll asks some voters how they voted as they leave.» What is an exit poll? | A room where the votes are counted | *A quick survey that guesses the result | A law about who can vote | A list of all the candidates
test: «Ireland uses the single transferable vote: voters rank candidates.» What do voters do in this system? | They choose only one candidate and stop | *They put the candidates in order | They throw away all their votes | They write a new law
test: What does voter turnout mean? | The rules for counting votes | The number of people who stand as candidates | The name of a political party | *How many people actually vote in an election
exam: What is a floating voter? | A voter who is too young to vote | A voter who always votes for the same party | A voter who never votes at all | *A voter who may change which party they vote for
